"""PythonEggs lessons: selectable explanations and working, editable Python examples.

Application version is inherited from main.py.
"""

LESSONS = [
    ('01 · Defining Functions',
     "WHAT YOU ARE LEARNING\nFunctions are named groups of instructions. You write a function once, then call it whenever you need that behavior. Python uses indentation, rather than braces, to identify the statements inside it.\n\nTHE SYNTAX\n    def calculate(first, second):\n        total = first + second\n        return total\n\n• def is a Python keyword meaning “define a function.” It is part of Python itself; you do not import it.\n• calculate is a name chosen by the programmer, not a built-in command. Names are case-sensitive. Use descriptive snake_case names.\n• (first, second) declares two PARAMETERS: local names for values the caller supplies. Parentheses are required even with zero parameters.\n• The colon (:) says an indented block follows. All lines in the function body must have a consistent indentation, conventionally four spaces.\n• total = first + second assigns the sum to a local variable.\n• return total sends that value back to the caller and ends this invocation. Without an explicit return, the function returns None.\n• calculate(8, 3) CALLS the function. The values 8 and 3 are ARGUMENTS; they become first and second for that call.\n\nWHY YOU SEE AN IMPORT IN THE EDITOR\nThe line “from tkinter import ttk” loads themed desktop UI controls so the example can display its result. tkinter is Python's standard Tk-interface library; ttk is its themed-widget module. This is not part of function syntax. The next lesson explains imports in full.\n\nIN PYTHONEGGS\nThe name preview refers to a frame already supplied by the application. ttk.Label(preview, ...) adds visible text inside that frame. print(...) writes to the Console / Errors area.\n\nTRY IT\nChange 8 and 3. Then change + to *. Finally, remove “return total” and see the result become None.",
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\ndef calculate(first, second):\n    total = first + second\n    return total\n\nanswer = calculate(8, 3)\nttk.Label(preview, text=f"Result: {answer}", font=("Sans", 18)).pack(pady=20)\nprint("Function returned:", answer)'),
    ('02 · Importing Modules',
     "WHAT IS AN IMPORT?\nAn import makes another Python module's code available to your file. A MODULE is usually a .py file (or a built-in module). A PACKAGE is an organized collection of modules. Importing saves you from rewriting commonly needed code.\n\nWHERE DO MODULES COME FROM?\n1. Built-ins and the STANDARD LIBRARY: Distributed with Python. Examples: math (math functions), random (pseudo-random values), pathlib (file paths), json (JSON data), and tkinter (desktop user interfaces). On Ubuntu, tkinter sometimes requires the separate operating-system package python3-tk.\n2. THIRD-PARTY packages: Installed separately for your Python environment, commonly with pip. Examples: numpy (numerical arrays), pandas (tables), matplotlib (charts), PySide6 (Qt desktop interfaces). They are not automatically available.\n3. YOUR OWN files: If you create helpers.py in your project, “import helpers” can load that module.\n\nWHAT IS “tkinter”?\nIt is the established NAME of Python's Tk interface module, not a Python keyword or a required naming pattern. Tk is the underlying GUI toolkit. We use it here because PythonEggs itself uses Tkinter. “ttk” means themed Tk widgets; it supplies controls such as ttk.Label and ttk.Button.\n\nTHREE IMPORT FORMS\n• import math — Load a module and keep its name. Call math.sqrt(81). sqrt means square root.\n• import tkinter as tk — Load tkinter but create the shorter local ALIAS tk. You could choose another alias, but tk is conventional. “as” is Python syntax; “tk” is your choice.\n• from tkinter import ttk — Import the ttk module from inside tkinter; then call ttk.Label(...) without the tkinter prefix.\n\nWHAT DOES THE DOT MEAN?\nA dot accesses a named attribute within something: math.sqrt is the sqrt function supplied by math; ttk.Label is the Label widget constructor supplied by ttk. The dot is not specific to imports.\n\nREAD THE EXAMPLE\nmath.sqrt(81) returns 9.0. tk.StringVar(...) creates a Tkinter-managed text variable that UI widgets can follow. ttk.Label(preview, textvariable=message) displays that variable; .pack(...) places the label in the window.\n\nHOW DO I FIND WHAT EXISTS?\nSee Python's official Standard Library Reference (docs.python.org/3/library/) for included modules. Use a package's documentation to learn what it provides. In a Python console, help(math) explains math; dir(math) lists its exposed names. You cannot assume a third-party library is installed just because you saw it in example code.\n\nTRY IT\nChange sqrt(81) to sqrt(144), then try math.factorial(5). Do not replace import math unless you also change every math. reference.",
     'import math\nimport tkinter as tk\nfrom tkinter import ttk\n\nvalue = math.sqrt(81)\nmessage = tk.StringVar(value=f"Square root: {value}")\nttk.Label(preview, textvariable=message, font=("Sans", 17)).pack(pady=20)\nprint("The math module supplied sqrt().")'),
    ('03 · Creating Windows',
     'WHAT IS A WINDOW?\nDesktop applications use a GUI (graphical user interface). A window contains WIDGETS—buttons, labels, text inputs, menus, etc. Python itself does not have window-building syntax: you use a GUI library such as tkinter (standard library) or PySide6 (third-party).\n\nWHAT ARE tk AND ttk?\n“import tkinter as tk” gives tkinter the local nickname tk. “from tkinter import ttk” accesses Tkinter\'s themed controls. ttk.Label is a label class, not a Python keyword.\n\nA STANDALONE TKINTER APP\n• root = tk.Tk() creates the root application window. root is a variable name you chose.\n• root.title("My App") changes the window-title text.\n• root.geometry("640x400") requests a window 640 pixels wide and 400 high. The x is literally part of the size string.\n• heading = ttk.Label(root, text="Hello") constructs a label belonging to root. text= is a keyword argument.\n• heading.pack(padx=25, pady=25) makes it appear. pack is Tkinter\'s layout manager; padx and pady specify horizontal and vertical spacing.\n• root.mainloop() enters Tk\'s event loop, allowing click and key events to be processed.\n\nWHY THE EDITOR LOOKS DIFFERENT\nPythonEggs already created the Tk root window and calls mainloop. Here, preview is an existing child frame, so we attach the label to preview. Creating a second tk.Tk() inside a lesson would not be the correct way to create an inline preview.\n\nA NOTE ON COMMENTS\nLines starting with # are comments. Python ignores them. The standalone-window lines in this lesson are comments so the lesson can run safely inside PythonEggs.\n\nTRY IT\nChange the label text and font size; adjust padx/pady. When building a separate program, the window setup and mainloop belong in that program\'s main.py.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\n# Standalone: root = tk.Tk()\n# Standalone: root.title("My App")\n# Standalone: root.geometry("640x400")\nheading = ttk.Label(preview, text="My Application", font=("Sans", 20))\nheading.pack(padx=25, pady=25)\n# Standalone: root.mainloop()'),
    ('04 · Buttons and Callbacks',
     'WHY BUTTONS NEED FUNCTIONS\nTkinter\'s ttk.Button is a widget. It cannot decide what your application should do when clicked: you provide a CALLBACK, which is an existing Python function that Tkinter can call later.\n\nFIRST, DEFINE THE FUNCTION\n    def on_click():\n        status.config(text="Clicked")\nThe keyword def, parentheses, colon, and indentation are Python syntax. on_click is your chosen function name. status is a variable referencing an existing label widget. config(...) is that widget\'s method for changing its settings.\n\nTHEN CREATE A BUTTON\n    button = ttk.Button(preview, text="Run Action", command=on_click)\n• ttk comes from Tkinter\'s standard-library themed widgets.\n• Button(...) is a constructor that makes a button widget.\n• preview is the parent frame provided by PythonEggs.\n• text= supplies its visible caption.\n• command= specifies what function runs when clicked.\n• command=on_click passes the FUNCTION REFERENCE. command=on_click() would call it immediately and pass the result, which is usually wrong here.\n• button.pack(...) gives it a position in its parent frame.\n\nHOW CALLBACKS WORK\nThe code first defines on_click, then constructs the status label and button. Clicking asks Tkinter to call on_click; it changes the label, and print writes to the PythonEggs console.\n\nTRY IT\nChange the button caption. Change the callback\'s status text. Then try command=on_click() to understand why the parentheses matter—and restore command=on_click afterward.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\ndef on_click():\n    status.config(text="The button was clicked!")\n    print("Callback executed")\n\nstatus = ttk.Label(preview, text="Waiting for a click")\nstatus.pack(pady=12)\nbutton = ttk.Button(preview, text="Run Action", command=on_click)\nbutton.pack(pady=8)'),
    ('05 · Variables and Types',
     'WHAT IS A VARIABLE IN PYTHON?\nA variable is a name that refers to a Python object (for example, a string or number). You do not declare its type separately in ordinary Python code: assigning an object binds the name to that object.\n\nSYNTAX AND TYPES\n• name = "PythonEggs" assigns a str (text string).\n• count = 4 assigns an int (whole number).\n• price = 2.75 assigns a float (floating-point number).\n• enabled = True assigns a bool (boolean value). True and False must be capitalized.\n• The equals sign (=) is assignment. Double equals (==) checks equality, normally in a condition.\n• type(count) returns the object\'s type. The built-in type needs no import.\n\nSTRINGS AND FORMATTING\nQuoted text is a string. An f-string begins with f before its quote: f"Count: {count}" replaces {count} with the variable\'s value. {price:.2f} formats a number to two decimal places; the formatting rule is inside the braces.\n\nSCOPE\nNames assigned inside a function are usually local to it. Names at the top of a module are module-level. A function can read outer names, but assigning to them has separate scope rules.\n\nORDINARY VARIABLES VS TKINTER VARIABLES\nA normal string (name = "A") and tk.StringVar are different. Tkinter\'s StringVar is a wrapper that a widget can track as it changes; it is only needed for particular GUI techniques.\n\nTRY IT\nChange each value and examine both the displayed string and the types printed below. Try count = "4" and notice that the displayed characters stay similar while its type changes.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\nname = "PythonEggs"\ncount = 4\nprice = 2.75\nenabled = True\nmessage = f"{name}: {count} eggs at ${price:.2f} each"\nttk.Label(preview, text=message).pack(pady=18)\nprint(type(name).__name__, type(count).__name__, type(price).__name__)\nprint("Enabled:", enabled)'),
    ('06 · Widget Layout: pack and grid',
     'WHY LAYOUT MANAGERS EXIST\nMaking a widget does not automatically place it on screen. In Tkinter you normally call pack(), grid(), or place() to decide where a widget goes. These are Tkinter methods, not Python keywords.\n\nPACK: ARRANGE RELATIVE TO NEIGHBORS\n• widget.pack() places a widget into its parent.\n• side="left" puts the next widget toward the left; side="top" stacks vertically.\n• padx=6 and pady=6 add horizontal/vertical outside spacing in pixels.\n• fill="x" permits expansion across available horizontal space; expand=True allows a widget to claim extra space.\n\nGRID: ARRANGE BY ROW AND COLUMN\n• widget.grid(row=0, column=1) positions a child at row 0, column 1.\n• Rows and columns are zero-indexed.\n• sticky="ew" can make the widget stretch east/west within its cell.\n\nFRAMES AND PARENTS\nA Frame is a container. In the example, row = ttk.Frame(preview) creates a child container; the buttons use row as their parent. A single parent must not mix pack and grid to manage its own direct children, but separate frames may use different managers.\n\nPYTHON SYNTAX IN THE EXAMPLE\nfor caption in ["Open", "Save", "Close"]: is a for loop over a list. caption is assigned each string; the indented ttk.Button line repeats.\n\nTRY IT\nChange side="left" to side="top"; change padx to 20. Try adding a fourth caption to the list.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\nrow = ttk.Frame(preview)\nrow.pack(pady=15)\nfor caption in ["Open", "Save", "Close"]:\n    ttk.Button(row, text=caption).pack(side="left", padx=6, pady=6)'),
    ('07 · Text Fields and Inputs',
     'WHAT INPUTS RETURN\nA text field accepts user input. Tkinter\'s ttk.Entry is a single-line editable widget, not a built-in Python data type. The contents returned by entry.get() are strings—even if the user typed digits.\n\nCREATING THE FIELD\n• entry = ttk.Entry(preview) creates the Entry and stores its widget reference in entry.\n• entry.insert(0, "PythonEggs") inserts starter text at index 0 (the beginning).\n• entry.pack(pady=10) makes the field visible.\n\nREADING THE CURRENT VALUE\n• entry.get() returns the current text as str.\n• entry.delete(0, "end") removes all text from the field.\n• int(entry.get()) converts to a whole number if the text is valid; otherwise raises ValueError.\n\nFOLLOW THE CALLBACK\nA function named display() reads the Entry and calls result.config(text=...). The button\'s command=display passes a reference to that function for later execution.\n\nTRY IT\nType a different value and click Display. Change the output f-string. Add entry.delete(0, "end") after updating the label to clear the field when the button is clicked.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\nentry = ttk.Entry(preview)\nentry.insert(0, "PythonEggs")\nentry.pack(pady=10)\nresult = ttk.Label(preview, text="Type a name")\nresult.pack(pady=8)\n\ndef display():\n    result.config(text=f"Name: {entry.get()}")\n\nttk.Button(preview, text="Display", command=display).pack()'),
    ('08 · Conditions: if, elif, else',
     'WHAT CONDITIONS DO\nPython\'s if / elif / else structure selects one of several possible INDENTED blocks based on conditions. The words if, elif and else are built-in Python keywords; no imports are needed.\n\nSYNTAX\n    if power > -40:\n        status = "Strong"\n    elif power > -70:\n        status = "Moderate"\n    else:\n        status = "Weak"\n\n• if checks the first condition.\n• elif means “else if”: it is checked only if earlier conditions were false.\n• else runs if no earlier branch was selected. There is no condition after else.\n• Each branch ends with a colon and contains indented statements.\n• > and < mean greater/less than; >= and <= include equality; == tests equality; != means not equal.\n• and, or, not combine or negate conditions. Python also treats many non-boolean objects as true or false depending on their contents.\n\nWHY ORDER MATTERS\nThe first successful branch wins. With power = -55, -55 > -40 is false, but -55 > -70 is true; status becomes Moderate. Reverse the tests carelessly and you may classify values incorrectly.\n\nTRY IT\nTry -10, -55, and -90; observe which branch runs. Then change the thresholds and predict the answer before refreshing.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\npower = -55\nif power > -40:\n    status = "Strong signal"\nelif power > -70:\n    status = "Moderate signal"\nelse:\n    status = "Weak signal"\nttk.Label(preview, text=status, font=("Sans", 18)).pack(pady=20)'),
    ('09 · Lists and Indexing',
     "PYTHON LISTS VS ARRAYS\nA list is Python's general-purpose ordered, changeable collection. When people coming from JavaScript or other languages say “array,” a Python list is often what they need. Specialized typed/numerical arrays also exist, including array from the standard library and NumPy arrays from the third-party numpy package.\n\nSYNTAX\n• readings = [12, 18, 15] creates a list. Square brackets are Python syntax here.\n• readings[0] gets the first element; Python starts counting indexes at zero.\n• readings[-1] gets the last element.\n• readings[1:3] gets indexes 1 and 2, stopping before index 3.\n• readings.append(21) calls a list method to add one element.\n• len(readings) is a built-in function giving element count; it needs no import.\n\nA LOOP OVER THE DATA\nfor index, reading in enumerate(readings): loops over pairs (position, value). enumerate is a built-in Python function. A new ttk.Label is created for each reading.\n\nTRY IT\nChange the list, add readings.append(99), or change how the display formats each element. Experiment with readings[0] and readings[-1] in the console print.",
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\nreadings = [12, 18, 15]\nreadings.append(21)\nfor index, reading in enumerate(readings):\n    ttk.Label(preview, text=f"Reading {index}: {reading}").pack(anchor="w", padx=20)\nprint("First:", readings[0], "Last:", readings[-1])'),
    ('10 · Loops and Iteration',
     'WHY LOOPS ARE COMMON\nA loop repeats a block instead of requiring copied statements. “for” and “while” are Python keywords. They are part of the language and do not need an import.\n\nFOR LOOPS\n• for item in collection: runs the indented body once for each item.\n• range(4) produces 0, 1, 2, 3; 4 is NOT included.\n• enumerate(collection) produces index/value pairs.\n• The variable named in for is assigned a new value on each iteration.\n\nWHILE LOOPS\n• while condition: keeps repeating while the condition is true.\n• Ensure the condition eventually becomes false; a never-ending loop in PythonEggs can freeze the interface.\n• break exits the nearest loop immediately.\n• continue skips the rest of the current iteration.\n\nREAD THE EXAMPLE\nfor index in range(4) creates four labels. index + 1 is used only to show human-friendly numbering from 1 to 4.\n\nTRY IT\nChange range(4) to range(7). Change index + 1 to index. Notice what starts at zero and what starts at one.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\nfor index in range(4):\n    ttk.Label(preview, text=f"Row {index + 1}").pack(anchor="w", padx=18, pady=3)\nprint("Loop finished")'),
    ('11 · Dictionaries',
     'WHAT A DICTIONARY IS\nA dictionary maps unique KEYS to VALUES, much like a JavaScript object used as a record. Python dictionaries are built-in and require no import.\n\nSYNTAX\n• sensor = {"name": "SDR", "gain": 20} creates a dictionary. Curly braces contain key: value pairs.\n• sensor["gain"] reads a value or raises KeyError if the key does not exist.\n• sensor["gain"] = 25 replaces its value.\n• sensor.get("missing", 0) returns 0 instead of raising KeyError if that key is absent.\n• sensor.items() supplies (key, value) pairs for iteration.\n\nPRACTICAL USES\nStore program settings, sensor data, parsed JSON records, and other named information. JSON is a text interchange format; Python dictionaries are actual in-memory objects and not identical to JSON text.\n\nREAD THE EXAMPLE\nThe for key, value in sensor.items(): line unpacks each pair into two local names. The indented label shows the pair.\n\nTRY IT\nAdd "mode": "FM" to the dictionary and modify gain. See that all fields appear without creating separate widgets manually.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\nsensor = {"name": "SDR", "frequency": 101.5, "gain": 20}\nsensor["gain"] = 25\nfor key, value in sensor.items():\n    ttk.Label(preview, text=f"{key}: {value}").pack(anchor="w", padx=18, pady=3)'),
    ('12 · Classes, self and __init__',
     "WHY CLASSES APPEAR IN APPLICATIONS\nA class is a blueprint for objects with related state and behavior. Tkinter's widgets are classes, so understanding classes explains code such as ttk.Button(...). Python classes are a language feature; no import is needed to define your own.\n\nSYNTAX\n• class Sensor: starts a class definition; the indented block contains its methods.\n• def __init__(self, frequency): defines the initializer called when you construct Sensor(101.5).\n• self is the conventional name for the current INSTANCE, the individual object being used. It is not a reserved Python keyword.\n• self.frequency = frequency stores data on that instance.\n• def describe(self): defines a method, a function belonging to this class.\n• sensor.describe() calls the method. Python supplies sensor as self automatically.\n\nWHAT IS __init__?\nThe double underscores are part of the special method name; pronounce it “dunder init.” It initializes a newly created instance. It should not explicitly return a value.\n\nCONVENTIONS\nClass names commonly use CapWords (SensorReading). Variables and functions commonly use snake_case (read_signal). These are style conventions, not requirements enforced by the interpreter.\n\nTRY IT\nChange Sensor to RadioSensor in both definition and construction. Add a second instance with a different frequency.",
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\nclass Sensor:\n    def __init__(self, frequency):\n        self.frequency = frequency\n\n    def describe(self):\n        return f"Frequency: {self.frequency} MHz"\n\nsensor = Sensor(101.5)\nttk.Label(preview, text=sensor.describe(), font=("Sans", 16)).pack(pady=20)'),
    ('13 · Error Handling',
     'WHY ERRORS SHOULD NOT BE HIDDEN\nPython reports problems using EXCEPTIONS. For example, int("abc") cannot produce an integer, so it raises ValueError. A try/except block lets your program handle expected errors rather than crashing unexpectedly.\n\nSYNTAX\n• try: begins the block that may fail.\n• except ValueError as error: catches this specific error type and binds the exception object to error.\n• else: optionally runs only if the try block succeeds.\n• finally: optionally runs whether the operation succeeded or failed.\n• Exception types such as ValueError are built into Python. You do not need to import them.\n\nWHY BE SPECIFIC?\nCatching only expected exceptions helps reveal programming mistakes. A bare except: can hide unrelated failures. For user inputs, catching ValueError around numeric conversion is appropriate.\n\nHOW THE EXAMPLE WORKS\nThe raw variable starts as text. int(raw) attempts conversion. If that fails, the except body displays a helpful message while print displays technical details in the console.\n\nTRY IT\nChange raw to "42" and compare with "not a number". Then try float("12.5") to see how decimal-number conversion differs.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\nraw = "not a number"\ntry:\n    result = f"Number: {int(raw)}"\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    result = f"Invalid integer: {raw}"\n    print("Caught:", error)\nttk.Label(preview, text=result).pack(pady=20)'),
    ('14 · Events and bind()',
     'WHAT AN EVENT IS\nDesktop GUIs respond to events such as mouse clicks and keyboard changes. Tkinter\'s event system is provided by the tkinter standard-library module; bind is a METHOD on Tkinter widgets, not a Python keyword.\n\nSYNTAX\n• entry.bind("<KeyRelease>", on_key) registers the on_key function for each key-release event.\n• <KeyRelease> is Tk\'s event pattern inside a Python string, not Python\'s comparison syntax.\n• def on_key(event): defines a callback that receives an event object.\n• event.widget refers to the widget receiving the event.\n• event.widget.get() fetches the current text because that widget is an Entry.\n\nDIFFERENCE FROM BUTTON COMMAND\nA button\'s command=on_click callback normally accepts no event argument. A function called through bind normally receives one event argument. Mixing these signatures may produce TypeError.\n\nREAD THE EXAMPLE\nEach time you release a key, on_key reads what is currently in the Entry and updates the output label.\n\nTRY IT\nType in the field. Change <KeyRelease> to <FocusOut> and click elsewhere to compare when updates happen.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\nentry = ttk.Entry(preview)\nentry.pack(pady=10)\noutput = ttk.Label(preview, text="Start typing")\noutput.pack(pady=8)\n\ndef on_key(event):\n    output.config(text=f"Typed: {event.widget.get()}")\n\nentry.bind("<KeyRelease>", on_key)'),
    ('15 · Working with Numbers',
     'PYTHON\'S BUILT-IN NUMBER TOOLS\nBasic arithmetic operators and functions such as sum, min, max, and len are built into Python. They do not require installing a numerical library. For specialized matrix math and large arrays, NumPy is an optional third-party package.\n\nOPERATORS\n• + addition; - subtraction; * multiplication; / ordinary division (returns a float).\n• // floor division; % remainder (modulo); ** exponentiation (power).\n• Parentheses (...) control grouping and evaluation order.\n\nCONVERSION\n• int("42") parses whole-number text.\n• float("12.5") parses decimal-number text.\n• Invalid text raises ValueError, which your program can handle with try/except.\n\nANALYZING READINGS\nreadings is a list. sum(readings) adds the values; len(readings) counts them; dividing calculates an average. max(readings) finds the largest. The : .2f portion inside f"{average:.2f}" is a format specification, not arithmetic.\n\nTRY IT\nChange the readings, predict the average, and inspect the result. Try replacing max with min. Remove a reading and see how len changes.',
     'from tkinter import ttk\n\nreadings = [12.5, 15.0, 18.25, 16.0]\naverage = sum(readings) / len(readings)\nmaximum = max(readings)\nttk.Label(preview, text=f"Average: {average:.2f}").pack(pady=12)\nttk.Label(preview, text=f"Maximum: {maximum:.2f}").pack()\nprint("Count:", len(readings))'),
    ('16 · Creating Charts with Canvas',
     'WHAT CANVAS IS\nTkinter\'s Canvas widget draws shapes, lines, and text in a desktop interface. Canvas is a class supplied by the standard-library tkinter module, not a built-in Python syntax element. This is useful for custom visualizations; for advanced graphs, matplotlib is a separate third-party library.\n\nUNDERSTANDING THE IMPORT\nimport tkinter as tk loads tkinter and names it tk locally. The nickname tk is conventional; it is not a keyword. tk.Canvas constructs a Canvas widget.\n\nSYNTAX\n• tk.Canvas(preview, width=360, height=185, bg="white") creates a drawing surface inside the existing preview frame.\n• canvas.pack(...) makes it visible.\n• canvas.create_rectangle(x1, y1, x2, y2, fill="steelblue") draws a shape given two opposite corners.\n• canvas.create_text(x, y, text="Hi") draws text at a point.\n• Coordinates start at (0, 0) in the top-left. x increases rightward; y increases downward.\n\nDATA TO DRAWINGS\nThe loop reads each list value, computes an x position and bar height, and draws bars to represent those numbers. Multiplying value by 2 scales the height; it is a visual choice, not a required Python rule.\n\nTRY IT\nChange the readings and observe the bar heights. Change the scaling factor or the color. If bars overflow, adjust the canvas height or scale.',
     'import tkinter as tk\n\nreadings = [15, 45, 24, 72, 36]\ncanvas = tk.Canvas(preview, width=360, height=185, bg="white")\ncanvas.pack(pady=10)\nfor index, value in enumerate(readings):\n    x = 15 + index * 65\n    canvas.create_rectangle(x, 160 - value * 2, x + 42, 160, fill="steelblue")\n    canvas.create_text(x + 21, 172, text=str(value))'),
]
